In that diagnostic, the fastest successful `stair` configuration required approximately 1.26 times the GLPK wall time. A separate primal-simplex diagnostic timed out on all four pilot-family instances (`pilot.ja`, `pilot`, `pilot.we`, and `pilot87`). These focused, dated repository records do not constitute a comprehensive solver benchmark: they report neither a geometric-mean runtime nor a representative MIP corpus and do not describe performance at the current revision. They support only the narrower conclusion that variation in local control policies had not resolved convergence on the selected difficult LP instances.

#strong[The relevant systems observation is that, on harder models, plausible local improvements did not reliably produce robust whole-solver behavior.] A pivot-rule modification cannot be evaluated solely from the validity of individual pivots; its system-level effect depends on degeneracy, numerical drift, refactorization frequency, and the resulting trajectories across an instance distribution. Presolve, cut generation, branching, primal heuristics, propagation, node selection, conflict analysis, and recovery strategies introduce further interactions that determine whole-search performance. Consequently, feedback about whole-solver quality is slow, noisy, and difficult to attribute because the relevant mechanisms interact through partially hidden dependencies. Directed search along one local dimension can therefore resemble a random walk in the higher-dimensional outcome space: improvement in a local proxy provides weak guidance about global solver quality.

Ralph has modular decomposition and benchmark gates, but these do not make its decisive outcomes locally decidable because the most important interfaces are behavioral and numerical rather than merely syntactic. Sound boundaries can expose and contain complexity, but they do not remove complexity intrinsic to the domain: faster implementation does not make numerical stabilization or combinatorial search easier. A basis update may satisfy local residual and structural checks yet accumulate damaging numerical error across iterations. A pricing change may reduce iteration count on one subset of instances while increasing degeneracy or numerical instability on another. A mathematically valid cut can still degrade whole-search performance through numerical effects or an unfavorable change to the search tree. A primal heuristic may find valid incumbents yet consume resources better allocated to node processing or other search mechanisms.

Ralph's local decidability could be improved by separating mathematical state, numerical linear algebra, LP control policy, and MIP search policy more explicitly. Candidate invariants include residual bounds, basis-validity checks, monotonic bound and progress accounting, deterministic replay, and explicit recovery contracts. Feedback could be strengthened through stratified benchmark suites, differential feasibility and objective checks, numerical telemetry, controlled ablations, fixed resource budgets, and per-instance regression distributions rather than a single aggregate score.

These measures can improve tractability and assurance, but they do not remove the problem's intrinsic difficulty. Competitive solver behavior depends on decades of accumulated, tightly coupled numerical and algorithmic engineering. Expert architectural judgment can identify missing mechanisms, reject unsound changes, and design better experiments. Yet it cannot reliably predict emergent search behavior or substitute for empirical tuning across heterogeneous instances. Ralph therefore marks a practical boundary: locally successful components and optimal solutions on smaller or easier models do not ensure reliable convergence and performance on larger or harder instances when whole-system behavior is emergent and feedback is costly.

= What determines agentic tractability

The complexity regimes suggest that the engineering value of agent assistance depends less on nominal project difficulty than on the scope and coupling of engineering decisions and the quality and latency of feedback. Keel and Hull are difficult systems, but their explicit contracts and compositional boundaries make many changes locally decidable. Sail raises the integration burden because model, representation, backend, phase, and memory dimensions form a support cross-product. On harder models, Ralph enters an emergent-outcome regime in which local improvements weakly predict whole-solver behavior and feedback is delayed and noisy.

Agent assistance can reduce the implementation-skill bottleneck without necessarily reducing the systems-engineering bottleneck. Broader access to code generation therefore does not imply broader access to the judgment required to engineer reliable complex systems. The systems-engineering expertise that remains necessary includes selecting sound decompositions, identifying consequential invariants, distinguishing representative evidence from local proxies, and recognizing when further implementation is counterproductive.

Decomposition, invariants, and feedback are coupled. Decomposition determines where invariants can be stated; invariants determine what tests must observe; and feedback indicates whether the resulting boundaries provide meaningful isolation or merely organizational convenience. In Sail, separating architecture rules, quantization, placement, and token behavior makes a mismatch easier to attribute. In Ralph, a factorization routine may pass residual checks, or a cut may be mathematically valid, even while whole-solver behavior deteriorates. In such emergent systems, local guarantees remain necessary, but they provide weaker evidence of system-level success.

Architectural judgment is the ability to choose boundaries and constraints under incomplete information. It supports agentic work by narrowing the search space, specifying forbidden behavior, selecting reference behavior, and recognizing when a locally attractive solution undermines system-wide simplicity. A productive loop proceeds iteratively: the engineer defines the frame, the agent explores implementations, tests produce evidence, and the frame is revised when that evidence contradicts its assumptions.

The cases suggest that coding systems can extend an implementation more readily than they can recover the architectural decisions and validation logic that shaped it. Repository contents may preserve implementation artifacts, design rationale, validation evidence, and even rejected alternatives, but a coding system may not reconstruct them as a coherent decision trajectory. Under these conditions, more capable coding systems may amplify a systems expert's impact by allowing that expert to direct substantially greater implementation throughput. This creates a potential asymmetry: implementation throughput may expand faster than the expertise available to evaluate whole-system quality.

Architectural judgment is itself bounded. A high volume of generated changes consumes review attention and can accumulate verification debt faster than an engineer can retire it. Human supervisors may choose an elegant but incorrect decomposition, preserve obsolete assumptions, or overfit validation to known cases. Agreement among coding agents that share models, context, or validation assumptions should not be treated as independent evidence. Architectural judgment can require token parity, authority confinement, or numerical telemetry, but it cannot establish them without representative validation evidence.

Implementation speed introduces a further risk. Early boundaries are hypotheses and should remain cheap to revise. Coding agents can rapidly create downstream consumers of an immature abstraction, increasing the cost of revising or removing it. This is premature architectural hardening: implementation throughput converts uncertainty into dependency before the contracts and feedback regime are stable. Temporarily delaying breadth can therefore increase long-run engineering progress by reducing later rework.

Alternative explanations remain plausible. The cases differ in maturity, expert attention, domain knowledge, reference implementations, and success criteria. Future coding agents may reason more effectively about long-range interactions. Consequently, the cases do not establish that architectural decomposition caused the more favorable outcomes or that emergent coupling caused the less favorable ones. They support the narrower claim that engineering work with current coding agents is more tractable when correctness is locally decidable, invariants are enforceable, and system-level feedback is timely and representative.

== Testable predictions

If local decidability is an important determinant of agentic tractability, the framework implies several predictions for future evaluation. First, agent-assisted engineering outcomes should be better predicted by change-context breadth, validation locality, and compositional reliability than by source-code volume alone. Second, architectural interventions that improve these properties should increase the fraction of locally accepted agent changes that subsequently pass system-level validation. Third, verification debt, measured by unresolved validation obligations and the delay between local acceptance and representative system-level evidence, should increase when agent-generated dependencies and cross-component assumptions accumulate faster than contracts, tests, and representative evidence can validate them. Fourth, while architectural judgment remains a binding constraint, improvements in implementation capability should yield larger gains in reliable engineering throughput under more experienced systems-engineering supervision than under less experienced supervision. Finally, systems with weak compositionality and delayed or noisy system-level feedback should exhibit smaller gains in reliable engineering throughput from improvements in component-level coding capability than systems with high local decidability.

= Engineering implications

The practical response is not simply to reduce the use of coding agents, but to direct scarce engineering attention toward architecture and validation. Before increasing implementation throughput, define a provisional decomposition, specify dependency constraints and lifecycle and ownership rules, and establish end-to-end acceptance tests. Treat each generated change as a hypothesis to be tested, and account for review, integration, maintenance, and future explanation as part of its cost.

Maintain an architecture ledger that records responsibility boundaries, forbidden dependencies, invariant ownership, and deliberate scope exclusions. Prefer interfaces that expose semantic operations rather than incidental backend details. Require each optimization proposal to identify its reference path, fallback behavior, affected execution phases, and validation gates. When feedback is sparse, delayed, or difficult to attribute, invest first in observability and representative benchmark design. If representative feedback cannot be obtained at acceptable cost, narrow the supported scope.

The framework also suggests a stopping rule. If implementation creates cross-component assumptions faster than contracts and tests can expose and validate them, additional throughput is increasing verification debt rather than engineering value. At that point, the appropriate response is to pause expansion and consolidate or delete code, or reduce scope, even when generating more code is inexpensive.

= Threats to validity

The four cases come from a single practitioner's work and differ in maturity, domain, and success criteria. The repositories provide observable artifacts and records, but attributing project outcomes to AI assistance or particular architectural choices remains interpretive. No matched versions of these projects developed without coding agents exist. The proposed regimes are analytical categories rather than measured complexity classes, and assigning the four projects to them depends on qualitative judgment. Because the same practitioner formulated the framework, directed the projects, interpreted their evidence, and assigned them to regimes, the classifications are vulnerable to confirmation bias and lack independent rating. Future studies should define assignment criteria in advance and use multiple independent raters to assess inter-rater agreement. The Ralph table summarizes selected repository diagnostics rather than a representative benchmark, so it cannot establish performance across LP and MIP workloads generally. Claims about Sail's proposed successor architecture describe intended scope rather than demonstrated performance. Documentation written or revised after implementation may incorporate lessons learned later, making the architecture appear more deliberate from the outset than it was.

The predictions derived in Section IV could be tested by comparing matched tasks under different architectural specifications, measuring review and rework alongside generated volume, and varying feedback latency and coverage. They should also vary the systems-engineering expertise available to direct and evaluate agents, testing whether improved coding capability widens or narrows the resulting productivity differences. Useful outcome measures include escaped defects, invariant violations, cross-module change breadth, performance regressions, and time to diagnose system-level failures.

#pagebreak()

= Conclusion

In these cases, current coding agents substantially expanded the amount of implementation one engineer could attempt. The cases are consistent with the hypothesis that this added implementation capacity is most valuable when decomposition converts global obligations into locally decidable tasks, invariants are explicit and enforceable, and system-level feedback is timely and representative. Among the cases, Keel provides the strongest retrospective evidence for this hypothesis. Hull offers less mature evidence from an architecture converging under similar principles. Sail provides a prospective test of the framework, while Ralph illustrates both successful optimization on smaller models and the failure of local improvements to ensure reliable convergence on larger, harder ones.

Coding agents therefore tend to amplify the architecture and constraints supplied to them. Architectural judgment supplies this structure by selecting boundaries, exclusions, invariants, and validation requirements. Yet supervisory attention is finite, expert judgments can be mistaken, and architectural judgment cannot substitute for empirical feedback in emergent systems. The practical dividing line for agentic tractability is not whether coding agents can implement components, but whether component-level correctness composes into observable whole-system quality. The cases therefore suggest that the limiting constraints in large AI-assisted software projects may shift away from code production and toward coherent decomposition, architectural judgment, and effective validation. Generating code is becoming cheap. Managing complexity is not.

